.TH DEBARK 1 "October 2026" "DebArk 0.1.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
debark \- install Debian packages on Arch Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B debark
.RI [ global-options ]
.RI command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
DebArk installs Debian package payloads on Arch Linux without executing Debian maintainer scripts. System-mode installs are registered with pacman as namespaced debark-app-PACKAGE packages; payload files stay under /opt/PACKAGE. User-mode installs stay in the user's home directory and do not register with pacman.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install PACKAGE.deb|URL
Analyze and install a local Debian package or direct HTTP(S) URL.
.B install also accepts the pacman-style -S and dpkg-style -i shortcuts.
.TP
.B remove PACKAGE
Remove a DebArk-managed package. System-mode packages are removed through pacman.
.B remove also accepts -R and -r.
.TP
.B list, info, search, files, verify
Inspect managed packages and package contents.
.B list accepts -Q or -l; info accepts -Qi or -s; search accepts -Qs; files accepts -Ql or -L; verify accepts -Qk or -V.
.TP
.B repair PACKAGE
Restore damaged application payload files from the recorded source package.
.TP
.B fix [--adopt]
Migrate older DebArk records and recover package entries from pacman's database. With --adopt, ask before registering detected legacy app directories.
.TP
.B fix-shortcuts
Check and restore the system and invoking user's DebArk command shortcuts. The system shortcut check also runs at DebArk startup and after pacman transactions.
.TP
.B snapshot PACKAGE
Save the current managed application tree as a local restore point.
.TP
.B rollback PACKAGE [SNAPSHOT]
Restore a saved application tree.
.TP
.B repo add|remove|list|sync|search|info|install
Manage Debian sources. Sync, search, info and install are experimental and require a trusted keyring and synced index data.
.TP
.B -Ss QUERY, -Si PACKAGE
Search or inspect packages in already-synced experimental Debian indexes.
.TP
.B cve --suite SUITE
Compare installed Debian packages with Debian Security Tracker data. Experimental.
.TP
.B update
Refresh local dependency metadata and pacman's file-provider database, then check the signed source release for a newer version or maintenance commit. If one is available, show its changelog notes and ask before installing it.
.TP
.B upgrade
Show newer versions available in already-synced experimental APT indexes, then check for a DebArk update. App versions are not replaced by this command; a DebArk update is offered separately.
.B upgrade also accepts the pacman-style -Qu shortcut. update accepts -Fy.
.SH APT COMPATIBILITY
The installer adds apt, apt-get, apt-cache, and dpkg command aliases in /usr/local/bin and the invoking user's ~/.local/bin. DebArk checks and repairs its aliases at startup; a pacman post-transaction hook checks the system aliases after changes to packages named apt or dpkg. They route supported operations to DebArk-managed applications. Repository operations require the experimental APT preview plus a configured, synced repository. The wrappers support common install, update, upgrade, remove, search, and query operations; dpkg queries are read-only, while dpkg -i routes local .deb packages through DebArk's install and replacement flow. apt-get install -f without package names runs DebArk inventory recovery. They do not implement every APT option or the full Debian dependency solver, and they never execute Debian maintainer scripts. Unsupported commands fail explicitly. The original pacman-managed commands remain in /usr/bin; use apt-real, apt-get-real, apt-cache-real, or dpkg-real to run those commands through DebArk. PATH aliases cannot intercept absolute paths such as /usr/bin/apt, and pkexec may discard /usr/local/bin from PATH; use /usr/local/bin/apt-get explicitly for those callers.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -u , " --user"
Install app files and records in the current user's paths. DebArk itself remains installed system-wide. Commands that need system access request it through sudo or doas when needed.
.TP
.B --arch-system
Explicitly require system-wide app installation and pacman package registration. It cannot be combined with --user; plain system installs use the same integration.
.TP
.BR -y , " --yes"
Skip DebArk confirmation prompts.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Show the planned install without changing the system.
.TP
.B --no-deps
Skip installing mapped Arch dependencies.
.TP
.B --snapshot
Save a local restore point after a successful install.
.TP
.B --sandbox
Use bubblewrap or Firejail for generated launchers. Networking is disabled, the app receives a temporary home, and the system filesystem is read-only. Some apps may not work; a missing runtime causes the launcher to exit.
.TP
.B --threads N
Set the parallel file-copy worker count from 1 through 16.
.TP
.B --json
Request JSON output for commands that support it.
.TP
.BR -q , " --quiet"
Suppress informational output where supported.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Show diagnostic details where supported.
.SH SAFETY
Debian maintainer scripts are never executed. Removal keeps files whose contents changed. Applications are not sandboxed unless the install used --sandbox and a supported runtime is available. Experimental APT sync verifies signed Release metadata with the keyring you provide; CVE reports may be incomplete or uncertain. Verify sources and findings before acting.
Shortcuts perform one DebArk action; combined pacman operations such as -Syu do not update Arch system packages.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/debark/db.json
System package database.
.TP
.I /var/lib/debark/pkgs/
Per-package manifests and saved source packages.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pacman (8),
.BR ar (1)
